1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:04,445
Researchers asked three popular
chatbots one simple question:

2
00:00:04,595 --> 00:00:06,950
What was the title of
Adam Kalai's dissertation?

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,850
ChatGPT answered, “Boosting,
Online Algorithms,

4
00:00:10,925 --> 00:00:13,025
and Other Topics in Machine Learning.”

5
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:15,100
Carnegie Mellon, 2002.

6
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:17,000
DeepSeek gave a different title,

7
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:19,200
at Harvard, in 2005.

8
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:22,760
Llama gave a third, at MIT, in 2007.

9
00:00:23,205 --> 00:00:24,705
Three polished answers.

10
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,240
Not one had the right title,
or even the right year.

11
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:28,725
And Adam Kalai?

12
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:30,270
He's the paper's lead author.

13
00:00:30,905 --> 00:00:35,255
Meanwhile, Google DeepMind reported
that a different language model reached

14
00:00:35,355 --> 00:00:39,080
gold-medal level at the
International Mathematical Olympiad.

15
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:41,415
So how can AI be that capable,

16
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:43,510
and still sound this sure
while being this wrong?

17
00:00:44,235 --> 00:00:46,760
Short answer: a chatbot
writes what's likely,

18
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:48,560
and likely isn't always true.

19
00:00:48,785 --> 00:00:50,890
To see why, let's take one answer apart.

20
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:53,310
Here's part of ChatGPT's answer.

21
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:55,625
A language model writes in tokens:

22
00:00:55,725 --> 00:00:56,750
chunks of text.

23
00:00:56,925 --> 00:00:58,000
Some are whole words.

24
00:00:58,125 --> 00:00:59,325
Some are fragments.

25
00:00:59,525 --> 00:01:01,290
“Kalai” becomes “Kal” and “ai.”

26
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:03,795
Each token is really a number.

27
00:01:03,945 --> 00:01:08,420
At every step, the model turns the
tokens so far into a numerical picture

28
00:01:08,445 --> 00:01:11,760
of the context, and scores
every possible next token.

29
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:14,695
Here, it's choosing the
last digit of the year.

30
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:17,470
One option gets picked, added to the end,

31
00:01:17,595 --> 00:01:19,900
and the loop runs again,
token after token.

32
00:01:20,405 --> 00:01:23,880
Because there's a pick, the same
question can come out differently.

33
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:26,930
Asked for Kalai's birthday,
“only if you know,”

34
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:29,830
one model gave three different
dates in three tries.

35
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:31,490
Here's the key part.

36
00:01:31,665 --> 00:01:35,165
Those scores measure how likely
a piece of text is to come next.

37
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:36,970
Not how likely it is to be true.

38
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:40,740
Modern chatbots add more:
instruction training,

39
00:01:40,890 --> 00:01:43,840
step-by-step reasoning,
sometimes web search.

40
00:01:43,965 --> 00:01:46,790
But the answer itself is
still written token by token.

41
00:01:47,445 --> 00:01:49,520
So why would the likely answer be wrong?

42
00:01:49,695 --> 00:01:51,100
Look at what the model had to learn.

43
00:01:51,455 --> 00:01:54,155
What a dissertation title
sounds like is everywhere:

44
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:57,105
“Methods,” “Algorithms,”
“Machine Learning,”

45
00:01:57,255 --> 00:01:58,155
again and again.

46
00:01:58,555 --> 00:02:00,630
Famous facts repeat, too.

47
00:02:00,855 --> 00:02:05,050
The paper notes that models rarely miss
something like Einstein's dissertation title.

48
00:02:05,405 --> 00:02:09,355
But one researcher's thesis title might
appear rarely in the training data.

49
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,380
Maybe once. Maybe never.

50
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:14,220
Like a birthday, there's no
pattern to work it out from.

51
00:02:14,690 --> 00:02:17,940
So the model fills the gap with
what a title usually looks like,

52
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:21,565
and the confidence comes
along for free. “Is entitled.”

53
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:23,740
No “I think,” no “maybe.”

54
00:02:23,965 --> 00:02:26,260
That tone isn't a readout
of what the model knows.

55
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:30,000
Here's ChatGPT's answer
next to the actual record.

56
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:31,100
Spot the difference?

57
00:02:32,815 --> 00:02:34,090
Same university.

58
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,690
The wrong year: it was 2001.

59
00:02:36,815 --> 00:02:38,040
And a different title.

60
00:02:38,190 --> 00:02:41,570
The invented details look
exactly as solid as the true one.

61
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:43,385
So why guess at all?

62
00:02:43,535 --> 00:02:45,060
Why not say, “I don't know”?

63
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:48,790
The researchers argue part of the
answer is how models get graded.

64
00:02:49,255 --> 00:02:50,905
Picture a ten-question quiz.

65
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:52,530
Right answer: one point.

66
00:02:52,705 --> 00:02:53,880
Wrong answer: zero.

67
00:02:54,105 --> 00:02:55,510
“I don't know”: also zero.

68
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,300
Two test-takers each know six answers.

69
00:02:58,425 --> 00:03:01,570
The honest one says “I don't
know” to the rest. Six points.

70
00:03:01,945 --> 00:03:03,845
The guesser takes a shot at all four.

71
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:08,020
With four choices each, on
average one lands. Seven points.

72
00:03:08,195 --> 00:03:11,070
The guesser wins, with three
confident wrong answers.

73
00:03:11,665 --> 00:03:12,940
Now change one rule.

74
00:03:13,115 --> 00:03:14,670
A wrong answer costs a point.

75
00:03:15,225 --> 00:03:16,950
The honest one: still six.

76
00:03:17,075 --> 00:03:20,930
The guesser: six, plus one, minus
three. Four. Bluffing loses.

77
00:03:21,485 --> 00:03:24,185
When the researchers checked
ten popular benchmarks,

78
00:03:24,335 --> 00:03:26,535
nine graded strictly right or wrong,

79
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:28,910
with zero credit for “I don't know.”

80
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:31,685
Train and rank models on tests like that,

81
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:32,730
and guessing pays.

82
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:36,460
Their fix: state the penalty
for wrong answers up front,

83
00:03:36,610 --> 00:03:38,785
so admitting uncertainty can win.

84
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,610
It's one explanation, not the whole story,

85
00:03:41,735 --> 00:03:43,270
but a surprisingly simple one.

86
00:03:43,955 --> 00:03:45,255
So what actually helps?

87
00:03:45,405 --> 00:03:47,105
Start with the unglamorous move:

88
00:03:47,255 --> 00:03:48,155
check the record.

89
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:50,350
Here's Kalai's actual thesis.

90
00:03:50,525 --> 00:03:53,675
“Probabilistic and On-line
Methods in Machine Learning.”

91
00:03:53,850 --> 00:03:56,010
Carnegie Mellon, May 2001.

92
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:59,470
And checking means more
than spotting a citation.

93
00:03:59,645 --> 00:04:02,320
Does the source exist, and
does it actually say this?

94
00:04:03,015 --> 00:04:07,265
Inside one Claude model, Anthropic
researchers found a default circuit for

95
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:08,565
“I can't answer.”

96
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:11,230
Recognizing a familiar
name switches it off.

97
00:04:11,585 --> 00:04:13,460
When that recognition misfires,

98
00:04:13,585 --> 00:04:16,335
when a name seems familiar
but the facts aren't there,

99
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:18,935
the model can produce a plausible guess.

100
00:04:19,185 --> 00:04:21,140
Knowing a name isn't knowing the facts.

101
00:04:21,665 --> 00:04:25,590
Search, retrieval, and longer reasoning
can help when they bring in the right

102
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:26,565
evidence.

103
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:30,065
Turning down the randomness
makes answers more consistent,

104
00:04:30,190 --> 00:04:32,215
not necessarily more correct.

105
00:04:32,415 --> 00:04:33,810
None of it is a guarantee.

106
00:04:34,485 --> 00:04:37,110
So why does AI sound
right when it's wrong?

107
00:04:37,285 --> 00:04:40,210
Because sounding right comes
from patterns in language,

108
00:04:40,335 --> 00:04:43,390
and being right takes evidence
the model doesn't always have.

109
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:47,300
So when an answer matters, don't
ask whether it sounds right.

110
00:04:47,525 --> 00:04:50,380
Ask: what's the evidence, and
does it actually say this?

111
00:04:51,135 --> 00:04:52,460
It works on chatbots.

112
00:04:52,585 --> 00:04:54,300
It works pretty well on people, too.
